CAT = "cscsHeights"; SEG = "HGT"
Q = [
("Under the Work at Height Regulations 2005, what counts as work at height?",
 "Anywhere a fall could injure you", ["Only work over 2 metres high", "Only work on scaffolds and roofs", "Only work above 10 metres high"],
 "Work at height is any place where a person could fall a distance liable to cause injury, including near an opening or excavation at ground level. There is no minimum height."),
("What is the first step in the work at height hierarchy?",
 "Avoid work at height", ["Use a fall arrest harness", "Provide a soft landing system", "Give workers extra training"],
 "First avoid work at height where reasonably practicable, then prevent falls with suitable equipment, then minimise the distance and consequences of a fall."),
("At an open edge, which fall protection should normally be chosen first?",
 "Guard rails and toe boards", ["Individual fall arrest harnesses", "A safety net below", "A warning sign at the edge"],
 "Guard rails and toe boards prevent the fall and protect everyone without relying on each person. Nets only reduce the consequences of a fall, and personal fall arrest comes lower still; a sign protects no one."),
("On a construction site, what is the minimum height of the top guard rail at an edge?",
 "950 mm", ["750 mm", "850 mm", "1,250 mm"],
 "For construction work the Work at Height Regulations require the main guard rail at least 950 mm above the edge, with an intermediate rail and a toe board."),
("What is the main purpose of a toe board?",
 "To stop things falling off the edge", ["To stop the scaffold from moving", "To give workers something to stand on", "To mark the edge of a walkway"],
 "Toe boards stop tools and materials being kicked off a platform onto people below, and help stop feet slipping under the rails."),
("How often must a scaffold used for construction work be inspected, at most?",
 "Every 7 days", ["Every 14 days", "Every 30 days", "Every 3 months"],
 "A scaffold must be inspected before first use, after any alteration or event likely to affect its stability, such as high winds, and at intervals not exceeding 7 days."),
("A scaffold you need to use has a missing guard rail. What should you do?",
 "Do not use it and report it", ["Use it but stay away from that side", "Fix it with timber yourself", "Use it if your job is quick"],
 "Only competent scaffolders may alter a scaffold. Do not use it until it is made safe and report the problem."),
("A scaffold tag shows the scaffold is not safe to use. What should you do?",
 "Stay off the scaffold", ["Use it with a harness on", "Use it only for light tools", "Remove the tag and use it"],
 "Tags show whether a scaffold has been inspected and is safe to use. Never use a scaffold tagged as unsafe or incomplete."),
("What should you do if you need a scaffold board moved to reach your work?",
 "Ask the scaffolders to move it", ["Move it yourself and put it back", "Lift it and replace it after", "Stand on the transoms instead"],
 "Removing boards, ties or rails can make a scaffold unsafe. Only competent scaffolders should make alterations."),
("When is it acceptable to use a ladder for work?",
 "Short, light, low-risk work", ["Any job lasting up to a day", "Whenever a scaffold is not booked", "Heavy work with large loads"],
 "Ladders are for low-risk, short-duration work where more suitable equipment is not justified. Longer or heavier work needs a platform such as a tower."),
("What is the correct angle for a leaning ladder?",
 "1 out for every 4 up", ["1 out for every 2 up", "1 out for every 8 up", "Straight up against the wall"],
 "The 1 in 4 rule gives about 75 degrees. Too steep and it can fall backwards; too shallow and the foot can slip."),
("How far should a leaning ladder used for access extend above the landing point?",
 "About 1 metre", ["About 300 mm", "About 2 metres", "Level with the landing"],
 "The ladder should extend about 1 metre above the landing so you have a handhold when getting on and off, unless there is another secure handhold."),
("Why must you not overreach while working from a ladder?",
 "The ladder could slip or tip", ["It wears out the rungs faster", "It makes the job slower", "It voids the ladder's guarantee"],
 "Keep your belt buckle within the stiles and keep three points of contact where possible. Move the ladder instead of stretching."),
("What should you check before using a ladder?",
 "Stiles, rungs and feet are undamaged", ["It is the longest ladder on site", "It has been painted recently", "It is the same make as the others on site"],
 "Do a pre-use check for bent or split stiles, damaged or missing rungs and worn or missing feet. Do not use a defective ladder."),
("Before climbing a mobile tower scaffold, what must you check?",
 "The wheels are locked", ["The tower is on soft ground", "The outriggers are removed", "The guard rails are taken off"],
 "Lock the castors before use and check the tower has been built correctly with guard rails, toe boards and any outriggers required."),
("When may a mobile tower scaffold be moved?",
 "When nobody and nothing is on it", ["When people on it hold on tight", "When it is moved very slowly by hand", "When the wheels are locked"],
 "Never move a tower with people or materials on it. Check overhead obstructions and ground conditions before moving it."),
("Why are fragile roofs so dangerous?",
 "They can give way underfoot", ["They are always slippery", "They are hard to reach safely", "They block mobile phone signal"],
 "Fragile roofs and rooflights can give way without warning. Falls through them are a major cause of death in construction."),
("You need to cross a fragile roof. What should you do?",
 "Use the planned safe system", ["Walk on the bolt lines only", "Step only on the rooflights", "Crawl to spread your weight"],
 "Work on or near a fragile surface needs a planned safe system, such as staging, covers and guard rails. Walking on bolt lines is not a safe method."),
("Who should use a fall arrest harness?",
 "Only those trained to use one", ["Anyone working over 2 metres", "Anyone working on a scaffold", "Only supervisors and managers"],
 "Harnesses must be correctly fitted, inspected and attached to a suitable anchor. Users need training, and a rescue plan must be in place."),
("Why must there be a rescue plan when harnesses are used?",
 "Hanging in a harness is dangerous", ["The harness may need cleaning", "The anchor point may need replacing", "The client may ask to see a copy"],
 "Someone suspended in a harness after a fall can lose consciousness from suspension trauma. They must be rescued quickly."),
("What should you do before using a harness and lanyard?",
 "Check them for damage", ["Wash them with solvent", "Lengthen the lanyard", "Tie a knot in the webbing"],
 "Check webbing, stitching, buckles and connectors before each use. Damaged equipment must be taken out of use."),
("Holes in floors should be protected by what?",
 "Fixed, marked covers", ["Loose sheets of plywood", "Warning tape only", "A sign on the nearest wall"],
 "Covers must be strong enough, fixed in place and clearly marked so they are not moved. Tape alone does not stop a fall."),
("You need to work from a mobile elevating work platform (MEWP). What must you have?",
 "Training for that type of MEWP", ["A full car driving licence", "A current scaffold inspection tag", "A hot work permit for the day"],
 "MEWP operators must be trained and competent for the type of platform. A harness with a short restraint lanyard is usually worn in boom-type MEWPs."),
("Why should materials not be thrown down from a height?",
 "They can hit people below", ["They will break on landing", "It is slower than carrying", "It wastes materials"],
 "Use a chute, hoist or lower materials in a controlled way. Never throw items from a scaffold or roof."),
("Which weather condition can make working at height unsafe?",
 "Strong winds", ["Light cloud", "Mild temperatures", "Low humidity"],
 "Strong winds can blow people and materials off edges, and ice and rain make surfaces slippery. Stop work at height when conditions make it unsafe."),
("Where should you stand when using a stepladder?",
 "Below the top steps, with a handhold", ["On the top platform with no handhold", "Straddling the top of the steps", "On a tool tray for extra height"],
 "Do not stand on the top two steps of a stepladder, or the top three of swing-back steps, unless there is a safe handhold. Keep three points of contact."),
("Why should a ladder not be footed on a loose surface?",
 "The foot can slip out", ["It makes the ladder too tall", "It damages the ground", "It is harder to see"],
 "Ladders must be on firm, level ground. Secure the ladder by tying it or using an effective stability device."),
("Who may put up, alter or take down a scaffold?",
 "A competent scaffolder", ["Anyone who will use it", "The site manager", "A labourer with a spanner"],
 "Scaffolds must be erected, altered and dismantled by competent people under supervision. Users must not change them."),
]
